# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
debug
target

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# MSVC Windows builds of rustc generate these, which store debugging information
*.pdb

# Generated by cargo mutants
# Contains mutation testing data
**/mutants.out*/

# devenv
.devenv*
devenv.local.nix
devenv.local.yaml

# RustRover
#  JetBrains specific template is maintained in a separate JetBrains.gitignore that can
#  be found at https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Global/JetBrains.gitignore
#  and can be added to the global gitignore or merged into this file.  For a more nuclear
#  option (not recommended) you can uncomment the following to ignore the entire idea folder.
#.idea/
#

.ralph/
.lake

# Kali fixture build cache (per-fixture compiled artifacts)
crates/kali_cli/tests/fixtures/.kali-cache/

# TEMPORARY `cargo test` TARGETS WRITTEN INTO THE TESTS TREE BY THE MIGRATION
# CAPTURE RUNNERS. Cargo discovers integration-test targets in
# `crates/kali_cli/tests/` and nowhere else, so a runner that has to execute
# pinned bytes through the real harness must write a `.rs` there; each removes
# its own in a `finally` (`t19b5_capture_run.py`, `t19b2_captures.py`,
# `batch{5a,6a,7a,7b,8a}` in tools/task-18-browser-pilot). A SIGKILL between
# the write and the `finally` leaves one behind, and a stray one is a target
# `cargo test` compiles and `scripts/test-gate.sh` counts. Ignoring the whole
# `zz_*` class -- the naming convention every one of those runners already
# follows -- means a stray can never be committed. The runners refuse to start
# when one is already present, so it also cannot be silently inherited.
crates/kali_cli/tests/zz_*.rs

# CPython bytecode caches. The migration gates are Python: `test-gate.sh
# --gates-only` and every `tools/migration/gen_task19_batch*.py` import
# sibling modules, so running the gates locally leaves `__pycache__/` beside
# tracked scripts. It is build output, never a source of truth.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The blast-radius counter's npm tree. `tools/blast-radius/package.json` and
# `package-lock.json` are committed and pin acorn/acorn-walk EXACTLY, so the
# tree is reproducible from them with `npm ci`; the tree itself is build output.
tools/blast-radius/node_modules/
